  step; [38;5;153msrc/[39m is an unrelated CommonJS Node entry point ([38;5;153mgreet[39m), and [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no scripts, dependencies, or test
  runner. So I'm switching to the architectural path: clarifying questions, then 2-3 approaches, then a design you
  approve section by section, then a written spec — before any code.

  First question, because it decides how a shared module can even be delivered.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether "across the app" means surviving full page loads across separate HTML documents, or moving between
  views inside one page. That decides whether we need real browser storage and a module system, or just a module held in
  memory.

  - [1mMultiple HTML pages[0m (a login page, then other pages with other forms) — every navigation destroys all JavaScript
    state, so the ID [3mmust[0m go into browser storage and every page must load the shared module. Forces the storage
    decision and a delivery mechanism (script tag, ES modules, or a bundler). This is the assumption your answer reads
    like.
  - [1mOne page, multiple views[0m — state lives in memory; storage is only needed to survive a refresh. A plain module and an
    optional cache. Much smaller, and you can add storage later without redoing the interface.
  - [1mNot decided yet[0m — then I'd design for multiple pages, since that's the superset: a store built for page reloads
    works fine in a single page, but the reverse means rewriting the interface later.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m tell me multiple pages if that's the plan, and if you genuinely don't know, pick "not decided" and
  I'll design for the superset. The cost of guessing wrong toward the small option is redoing the interface every
  consumer depends on.
